  "status": "complete",
  "costUsd": null,
  "content": "Owen is stronger than the Division I average on short approaches. He is well behind it from 125 yards and out, and his overall approach play is the lowest on the team.\n\n- **50-125 yd proximity:** He averages 23.77 ft, against 28 ft for the Division I average. That is the best on the team, whose average is 28.61 ft. The Tour figure is 18 ft.\n- **125-175 yd proximity:** He averages 50.58 ft, against 38 ft for the Division I average. That is the lowest on the team, whose average is 45.42 ft. The Tour figure is 30 ft.\n- **175+ yd proximity:** He averages 94.65 ft, against 55 ft for the Division I average. The team average is 79.89 ft, and the Tour figure is 45 ft.\n- **Greens in regulation:** He hit 61.11% over his last ten rounds, which is 144 holes. That is slightly above the Division I average of 60%. It is below the team average of 66.25% and the Tour's 66%.\n- **Strokes gained on approach:** He is at -4.16 per round over that same window. The window holds only 8 recorded rounds, not ten. There is no college figure for this metric. The team average is -2.33, and he is last of 7 on the team. The Tour baseline is 0.\n\nThe proximity figures come from all his recorded rounds, refreshed nightly, so they cover a longer span than the ten-round window.\n\nThe loss is in the mid and long irons, not the wedges. The proximity gaps to the Division I average are 12.58 ft from 125-175 yd and 39.65 ft at 175+ yd. A practice block on those distances would target it better than more wedge work.",
